SALT INDUSTRY COOPERATIVE ACT

CHAPTER I GENERAL PROVISIONS

Article 1 (Purpose)
The purpose of this Act is to contribute to balanced development of the national economy by promoting sound development of the salt industry and improving the welfare of its members through an autonomous cooperative organization of salt manufacturers. <Amended on November 22, 2011>

Article 2 (Definitions)
The terms used in this Act are defined as follows: <Amended on November 22, 2011>
1. "Salt industry" means engaging in the business of manufacturing salt or brine defined in Article 2 of the Salt Industry Promotion Act, or re-manufacturing or processing salt;
2. "Salt manufacturer" means a salt manufacturer defined in Article 2, subparagraph 14 of the Salt Industry Promotion Act;
3. "Cooperative" means a salt industry cooperative established under this Act.

Article 3 (Prohibition of use of similar names)
No person other than a cooperative shall use the name "salt industry cooperative" or any similar name.

Article 4 (Legal personality)
(1) A cooperative shall be a corporation.
(2) A cooperative shall establish its principal office and may establish branch offices wherever necessary, as prescribed by its articles of incorporation.
(3) The territory of a cooperative shall encompass the whole country.

Article 5 (Application of other Acts mutatis mutandis)
Except as provided in this Act, the provisions of the Civil Act concerning incorporated associations shall apply mutatis mutandis to a cooperative.

CHAPTER II COOPERATIVES

SECTION 1 ESTABLISHMENT

Article 6 (Establishment)
(1) To establish a cooperative, at least 20 persons qualified for membership shall act as promoters, prepare articles of incorporation, obtain a resolution of the inaugural general meeting, and obtain authorization from the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries. <Amended on February 29, 2008; December 19, 2008; March 23, 2013>
(2) A cooperative shall come into existence upon registration of its establishment at the location of its principal office.

Article 7 (Matters to be stated in articles of incorporation)
(1) The articles of incorporation of a cooperative shall state the following:
1. Objectives;
2. Name;
3. Area of business;
4. Projects;
5. Locations of the principal office and branch offices;
6. Qualifications for membership;
7. Admission, withdrawal, and expulsion of members;
8. General meetings, the board of directors, and delegates;
9. Number of officers and their appointment and dismissal;
10. Value of one unit of capital contribution and method of payment;
11. Assessment of expenses and collection of penalty charges;
12. Fiscal year and accounting;
13. Disposal of surplus and treatment of losses;
14. Limits, types, and accumulation methods of reserves and provisions;
15. Standards for, and inspection of, products;
16. Election campaigning by officers and delegates, and the cooperative election management committee;
17. Use of the cooperative's projects by non-members;
18. Duration of existence or grounds for dissolution;
19. Method of public notice;
20. Other matters necessary for operating the cooperative.
(2) In addition to the matters specified in paragraph (1), the articles of incorporation may state the following: <Amended on December 27, 2022>
1. Where persons making contributions in kind have been designated, their names and addresses; the types, quantities, and values of property contributed; the number of capital contribution units allotted in return; and the conditions of any repurchase agreement;
2. Where property is agreed to be acquired at the time of establishment of the cooperative, the type, quantity, and value of such property and the transferor's address and name;
3. Addresses and names of the president, directors, and auditors at the time the cooperative is established.
(3) To amend its articles of incorporation, a cooperative shall obtain a resolution of the general meeting and authorization from the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries. <Amended on February 29, 2008; December 19, 2008; March 23, 2013>

SECTION 2 MEMBERS

Article 8 (Qualifications for membership)
A person who has obtained permission for the business of manufacturing salt under Article 23 of the Salt Industry Promotion Act or has reported the business of manufacturing or processing salt under Article 37 of the Food Sanitation Act may become a member of a cooperative.

Article 9 (Capital contributions and liability)
(1) Each member shall hold at least one capital contribution unit, and no member may hold more than 20/100 of the total number of capital contribution units.
(2) The value of each capital contribution unit shall be uniform and shall be prescribed by the articles of incorporation.
(3) A member may not assert a setoff against the cooperative concerning payment of the member's capital contribution.
(4) Where a cooperative suspends business, discontinues part of its business, or falls under any other circumstance prescribed by its articles of incorporation, it may reduce the number of its capital contribution units only at the end of a fiscal year.
(5) The liability of a member shall be limited to the amount of that member's capital contribution.

Article 10 (Assessment of expenses)
(1) A cooperative may assess expenses and penalty charges against its members as prescribed by its articles of incorporation.
(2) A member may not assert a setoff against the cooperative in paying expenses and penalty charges under paragraph (1).

SECTION 3 ORGANS

Article 11 (General meeting)
(1) Each cooperative shall have a general meeting.
(2) The general meeting shall consist of members and shall be convened by the president as prescribed by the articles of incorporation. <Amended on December 27, 2022>
(3) A regular general meeting shall be convened once every year at the time prescribed by the articles of incorporation, and an extraordinary general meeting may be convened whenever necessary.
(4) Except as otherwise provided in this Act, a general meeting shall open with the attendance of a majority of all members and adopt resolutions with the affirmative votes of a majority of members present: Provided, That the matters specified in Article 12, subparagraphs 2 and 3 shall be resolved with the attendance of a majority of all members and the affirmative votes of at least two thirds of those present.

Article 12 (Matters requiring resolution of the general meeting)
The following matters shall require a resolution of the general meeting: <Amended on December 27, 2022>
1. Amendment of the articles of incorporation;
2. Dissolution, merger, or division;
3. Expulsion of members;
4. Election and removal of officers;
5. Use of statutory reserves or business reserves;
6. Enactment, amendment, or repeal of regulations;
7. Formulation and revision of business plans and revenue and expenditure budgets;
8. Methods of assessing expenses and collecting penalty charges;
9. Borrowing and repayment of funds necessary for projects;
10. Settlement reports;
11. Acquisition, alteration, or transfer of fixed assets, or creation of other real rights therein;
12. Acts creating new obligations or extinguishing rights beyond those specified in business plans and revenue and expenditure budgets;
13. Other matters deemed necessary by the president or the board of directors.

Article 13 (Requests to convene a general meeting)
(1) Members may request the president to convene a general meeting by submitting a written statement specifying its purpose and reasons with the consent of at least 10/100 of all members. <Amended on December 27, 2022>
(2) Upon receipt of a request under paragraph (1), the president shall convene a general meeting within two weeks. <Amended on December 27, 2022>
(3) If the president fails to convene a general meeting within the period specified in paragraph (2) without good cause, an auditor shall convene it within five days. In such cases, the auditor shall perform the duties of chairperson. <Amended on December 27, 2022>
(4) If the auditor fails to convene a general meeting within the period specified in paragraph (3), a representative of the members who requested the meeting under paragraph (1) shall convene it. In such cases, that representative shall perform the duties of chairperson.

Article 14 (Notices and demands to members)
(1) When a cooperative gives notice or a demand to a member, it shall send the notice or demand to the address or residence of the member entered in the membership register.
(2) Notice of a general meeting shall be given by dispatching a notice stating the purposes of the meeting and other relevant matters at least seven days before the meeting opens: Provided, That if a general meeting is reconvened for the same purposes, notice shall be given no later than the day preceding the meeting.

Article 15 (Restrictions on voting rights)
(1) A general meeting may resolve only matters notified pursuant to Article 14 (2): Provided, That this shall not apply to urgent matters other than those specified in Article 12, subparagraphs 1 through 4 if a majority of all members attend and at least two thirds of those present vote in favor.
(2) A member shall not participate in voting on a matter in which the interests of that member conflict with those of the cooperative.
(3) With the consent of at least 3/100 of the members, a member may submit a written proposal to the president, at least 30 days before a general meeting, requesting that specific matters be included in the agenda of the meeting (hereinafter referred to as a "member proposal" in this paragraph). Unless the member proposal violates any statute or the articles of incorporation, it shall be included on the agenda; and if the proposer so requests, the proposer shall be afforded an opportunity to explain the proposal at the general meeting. <Amended on December 27, 2022>

Article 16 (Council of delegates)
(1) A cooperative may establish a council of delegates to replace the general meeting, as prescribed by its articles of incorporation.
(2) The council of delegates shall consist of delegates, and its operation shall be governed by the articles of incorporation.
(3) The council of delegates may resolve matters specified in Article 12, subparagraph 1 and subparagraphs 4 through 13: Provided, That this shall not apply where otherwise provided in this Act.
(4) The provisions concerning the general meeting shall apply mutatis mutandis to the council of delegates: Provided, That the voting rights of delegates may not be exercised through proxies.

Article 17 (Delegates)
(1) Delegates shall be elected by members from among the members.
(2) The term of office of a delegate shall be three years: Provided, That if the term expires before a regular general meeting concerning the final settlement of accounts during that term, the term shall be extended until the close of the regular general meeting.
(3) Except where the number of vacancies among delegates reaches at least one third of the total number of delegates, by-elections to fill vacancies shall be held immediately after the next regular general meeting: Provided, That the term of a delegate elected in a by-election shall be the remainder of the predecessor's term.
(4) A delegate may not concurrently serve as an officer or employee of the cooperative.

Article 18 (Board of directors)
(1) A cooperative shall have a board of directors.
(2) The board of directors shall consist of the president and directors. <Amended on December 27, 2022>
(3) The president shall convene and chair meetings of the board of directors. <Amended on December 27, 2022>
(4) Resolutions of the board of directors shall be adopted with the attendance of a majority of all directors and the affirmative votes of a majority of those present: Provided, That resolutions may be adopted in writing as prescribed by the articles of incorporation.
(5) Minutes shall be prepared for resolutions of the board of directors and shall be signed and sealed by the chairperson and the directors present.
(6) Matters necessary for operating the board of directors shall be prescribed by the articles of incorporation.

Article 19 (Matters requiring resolution of the board of directors)
(1) The board of directors shall resolve the following matters: <Amended on December 27, 2022>
1. Matters to be submitted to the general meeting;
2. Enactment, amendment, and repeal of operating regulations;
3. Transfers of appropriations between sections and items of the revenue and expenditure budget;
4. Limits on borrowings;
5. Collective contracts;
6. Matters delegated by the general meeting;
7. Other matters deemed necessary by the president or at least one third of the directors.
(2) Article 15 (2) shall apply mutatis mutandis to the board of directors.

Article 20 (Officers)
(1) A cooperative shall have not fewer than nine and not more than 12 directors, including one president, and two auditors. At least half of the directors shall be members of the cooperative. <Amended on December 27, 2022>
(2) The president, directors, and auditors shall serve on a non-standing basis: Provided, That a cooperative may appoint up to two directors, including the president, to serve on a standing basis (hereinafter referred to as "standing directors"), as prescribed by its articles of incorporation. <Amended on December 27, 2022>
(3) The president shall be elected by direct vote of the members from among members who have knowledge and experience in the salt industry. <Amended on December 27, 2022>
(4) Officers other than the president shall be elected at a general meeting: Provided, That standing directors shall be elected at the general meeting from among persons who have extensive professional knowledge and experience in the cooperative's affairs and meet requirements prescribed by Presidential Decree, upon the president's recommendation made with the consent of the board of directors. <Amended on December 27, 2022>
(5) Officers other than standing officers shall serve in an honorary capacity, but may be reimbursed for actual expenses, such as travel expenses for attending meetings and investigation expenses, within the budget as prescribed by the articles of incorporation.
(6) Matters necessary for election and recommendation of officers other than those provided in this Act shall be prescribed by the articles of incorporation.

Article 21 (Duties of officers)
(1) The president shall represent the cooperative and exercise general control over its affairs. <Amended on December 27, 2022>
(2) Standing directors shall assist the president and act on behalf of the president if the president is incapacitated or the office becomes vacant, as prescribed by the articles of incorporation. <Amended on December 27, 2022>
(3) The president shall chair the general meeting, council of delegates, and board of directors. <Amended on December 27, 2022>
(4) If the president and standing directors cannot perform their duties because their offices are vacant, they are detained, or they are continuously hospitalized for at least 30 days in a medical institution under the Medical Service Act, or for similar reasons, non-standing directors (excluding directors who are not members) shall act on their behalf in the order prescribed by the articles of incorporation. <Amended on December 27, 2022>
(5) Auditors shall audit the cooperative's property and accounts and report the findings to the general meeting.
(6) If an auditor discovers irregularities concerning the cooperative's property or accounts, the auditor shall report them to the general meeting; and if it is necessary to report them promptly, the auditor may request the president to convene a general meeting or convene the meeting directly, as prescribed by the articles of incorporation. <Amended on December 27, 2022>
(7) Auditors may attend the general meeting, council of delegates, and board of directors and express their opinions.
(8) Articles 412-4, 413, and 413-2 of the Commercial Act shall apply mutatis mutandis to the duties of auditors.

Article 22 (Exceptions to representation of the cooperative)
(1) When a cooperative concludes a contract with its president or a director, an auditor shall represent the cooperative. <Amended on December 27, 2022>
(2) Paragraph (1) shall apply to litigation between the cooperative and its president or a director. <Amended on December 27, 2022>
(3) The president may appoint an agent from among directors or employees to perform judicial or extrajudicial acts concerning the cooperative's affairs. <Amended on December 27, 2022>

Article 23 (Terms of office of officers)
(1) The term of office of the president and directors shall be four years, and the term of office of auditors shall be three years. <Amended on December 27, 2022>
(2) If the term of an officer expires before a regular general meeting concerning the final settlement of accounts during that term, the term may be extended until the close of the regular general meeting.

Article 24 (Restrictions on qualifications of officers)
(1) None of the following persons may become an officer of a cooperative: <Amended on March 18, 2014; December 31, 2018>
1. A person who is not a national of the Republic of Korea;
2. A minor, a person under adult guardianship, or a person under limited guardianship;
3. A person who has been declared bankrupt and has not been reinstated;
4. A person whose qualifications have been lost or suspended by a court judgment or under another Act;
5. A person for whom three years have not elapsed since completion of execution (including cases deemed completed) of a sentence of imprisonment without labor or a heavier punishment, or since exemption from execution of such sentence;
6. A person for whom five years have not elapsed since the date of a disposition requiring replacement or dismissal under Article 51 (1);
7. A person under a suspended execution of a sentence of imprisonment without labor or a heavier punishment whose suspension period has not expired;
8. A person subject to suspension of the pronouncement of a sentence of imprisonment without labor or a heavier punishment during the period of such suspension;
9. A person for whom four years have not elapsed since being sentenced to a fine of at least one million won for an offense specified in Article 59 (1) through (3);
9-2. A person who has committed an offense specified in Article 303 of the Criminal Act or Article 10 of the Act on Special Cases concerning the Punishment of Sexual Crimes and for whom two years have not elapsed since a fine of at least three million won for that offense became final and conclusive;
10. A person who was elected as an officer under this Act but whose election was invalidated or revoked for a reason attributable to that person, and for whom five years have not elapsed since the invalidation or revocation became final and conclusive;
11. A person who, on the date of the public announcement of an election, is delinquent in repaying debts to the relevant cooperative in excess of the amount and period prescribed by the articles of incorporation.
(2) If any ground under paragraph (1) is discovered or arises in relation to an officer, the officer shall retire from office.
(3) Acts in which an officer who retires under paragraph (2) participated before retirement shall remain valid.

Article 24-2 (Separate sentencing of fines)
Notwithstanding Article 38 of the Criminal Act, where a fine is imposed for concurrent crimes consisting of an offense specified in Article 24 (1) 9 or 9-2 and another offense, the fine for each such offense shall be pronounced separately.

Article 25 (Prohibition on concurrent offices)
An officer may not concurrently serve as an employee of the cooperative, and a standing director may not concurrently hold a position in any other public or private undertaking outside the cooperative.

Article 26 (Duties and liabilities of officers)
(1) Officers of a cooperative shall comply with this Act, orders issued under this Act, and its articles of incorporation, and shall faithfully perform their duties.
(2) Officers shall be jointly and severally liable to compensate the cooperative for damage caused by an act in violation of any statute or the articles of incorporation, or by neglect of duty, in performing their duties.
(3) Officers shall be jointly and severally liable to compensate a third party for damage caused intentionally or by gross negligence in performing their duties.
(4) Where an act under paragraph (2) or (3) results from a resolution of the board of directors, directors who voted in favor of the resolution shall also be jointly and severally liable for compensation. In such cases, a director who participated in the resolution and whose objection is not recorded in the minutes shall be presumed to have voted in favor.
(5) Paragraphs (2) and (3) shall also apply to damage caused to the cooperative or a third party by an officer's false settlement report, registration, or public notice.

Article 27 (Removal of officers)
(1) The president shall be removed by a vote of a majority of all members and the affirmative votes of a majority of voting members, following a resolution of the council of delegates. Paragraph (2) shall apply mutatis mutandis to the resolution of the council of delegates. <Amended on December 27, 2022>
(2) An officer elected by the council of delegates may be removed by a resolution passed with the attendance of a majority of delegates and the affirmative votes of at least two thirds of the delegates present, upon a request by at least one third of the delegates.
(3) Members may request the removal of an officer at a general meeting with the consent of at least one fifth of all members. In such cases, the general meeting shall adopt a resolution with the attendance of a majority of all members and the affirmative votes of a majority of those present.
(4) Before adopting a resolution on removal, the cooperative shall notify the officer concerned of the grounds for removal and afford the officer an opportunity to state an opinion at the general meeting or council of delegates.

Article 28 (Restrictions on election campaigns)
(1) No person shall commit any of the following acts for the purpose of having that person or any specified person elected, causing such a person to be elected, or preventing such a person from being elected as an officer or delegate of the cooperative:
1. Offering, expressing an intention to offer, or promising to offer money, goods, entertainment, other pecuniary benefits, or a public or private position to a member (including a person who has applied for membership; hereinafter the same shall apply in this Article) or a member's family (meaning the member's spouse; lineal ascendants, lineal descendants, and siblings of the member or spouse; and spouses of the member's lineal ascendants, lineal descendants, and siblings);
2. Committing an act under subparagraph 1 toward a prospective candidate or candidate to prevent the person from becoming a candidate or to induce a candidate to withdraw;
3. Receiving benefits or positions under subparagraph 1 or 2, or accepting an offer of such benefits or positions;
4. Requesting or arranging provision of benefits or positions under subparagraph 1 or 2.
(2) A person seeking to become an officer or delegate may not visit voters from house to house or gather voters at any specific place for election campaigning between the date on which the election date is announced and the election date.
(3) No person shall publish false facts or publicly disparage a candidate by stating facts through speeches, posters, or any other means in connection with an election of officers or delegates of the cooperative.
(4) No person shall conduct election campaigning concerning an election of officers or delegates by any means other than those prescribed in the articles of incorporation from among the following:
1. Posting campaign posters;
2. Distribution of election bulletins;
3. Distribution of small printed materials;
4. Holding joint campaign speeches or public debates;
5. Soliciting support by telephone or computer communications.
(5) Methods, standards, and other matters necessary for election campaigns under paragraph (4) shall be prescribed by the articles of incorporation.

Article 29 (Composition and operation of cooperative election management committee)
(1) A cooperative shall establish and operate a cooperative election management committee as prescribed by its articles of incorporation to manage elections of officers or delegates fairly.
(2) Where the cooperative election management committee manages elections of officers or delegates under paragraph (1), Article 272-2 of the Public Official Election Act and Article 14-2 of the Election Commission Act shall apply mutatis mutandis to regulation and investigation of violations of this Act.

Article 30 (Application of the Commercial Act mutatis mutandis)
Articles 382 (2), 385 (2) and (3), 386 (1), and 402 through 408 of the Commercial Act shall apply mutatis mutandis to officers. In such cases, "a shareholder holding shares equivalent to at least 3/100 of the total issued shares" in Article 385 (2) of the Commercial Act shall be deemed to mean "a member with the consent of at least 3/100 of the members," and "a shareholder holding shares equivalent to at least 1/100 of the total issued shares" in Articles 402 and 403 (1) of that Act shall be deemed to mean "a member with the consent of at least 1/100 of the members."

Article 31 (Duties of employees)
(1) Employees of a cooperative may attend the general meeting, council of delegates, and board of directors and express their opinions.
(2) Articles 11 (1) and (3), 12, 13, and 17 of the Commercial Act shall apply mutatis mutandis to employees.

SECTION 4 PROJECTS

Article 32 (Projects)
(1) To achieve its objectives, a cooperative shall conduct all or some of the following projects:
1. Guidance and coordination concerning members' businesses;
2. Research, investigation, dissemination, and publicity concerning members' businesses;
3. Purchasing, storage, and sales projects for members, joint projects relating thereto, and agency services;
4. Arrangement of loans of business funds to members;
5. Borrowing of funds and raising of funds for the cooperative's own projects;
6. Mutual aid projects for members;
7. Establishment of facilities for joint use to promote members' businesses;
8. Projects entrusted by the Government or public organizations;
9. Incidental projects related to those specified in subparagraphs 1 through 8.
(2) To conduct projects under paragraph (1), a cooperative may raise and operate funds to cover business losses and bad debts, as prescribed by its articles of incorporation.
(3) Before accepting entrustment of a project under paragraph (1) 8, a cooperative shall conclude an entrustment contract with the relevant institution.
(4) Articles 67 through 71 of the Commercial Act shall apply mutatis mutandis to purchasing and sales projects under paragraph (1) 3, and Articles 155 through 168 of that Act shall apply mutatis mutandis to storage projects.

Article 33 (Regulations on mutual aid projects)
(1) A cooperative intending to conduct mutual aid projects under Article 32 (1) 6 shall establish regulations on mutual aid projects and obtain approval from the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries. The same shall apply to any amendment to those regulations. <Amended on February 29, 2008; December 19, 2008; March 23, 2013>
(2) The regulations on mutual aid projects under paragraph (1) shall include matters concerning implementation of mutual aid projects, mutual aid contracts and premiums, accumulation of liability reserves and other reserves, and other matters as prescribed by the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries. <Amended on February 29, 2008; December 19, 2008; March 23, 2013>

Article 34 (Borrowings)
A cooperative may borrow funds necessary to conduct projects under Article 32 (1).

Article 35 (Use of projects by non-members)
A cooperative may allow non-members to use its projects as prescribed by its articles of incorporation, insofar as the interests of members are not adversely affected: Provided, That such use by non-members in the relevant fiscal year may be restricted as prescribed by the articles of incorporation.

SECTION 5 MANAGEMENT

Article 36 (Fiscal year and division of accounts)
(1) The fiscal year of a cooperative shall be prescribed by its articles of incorporation.
(2) The accounts of a cooperative shall be divided into general accounts and special accounts.
(3) Special accounts shall be established as prescribed by the articles of incorporation when operating a particular project, holding and operating particular funds, or otherwise when necessary to distinguish such accounts from general accounts.
(4) Matters necessary for accounting standards of a cooperative shall be prescribed by its articles of incorporation.

Article 37 (Business plans and revenue and expenditure budgets)
A cooperative shall prepare a business plan and a revenue and expenditure budget for each fiscal year and obtain a resolution of its general meeting at least one month before the commencement of that fiscal year. The same shall apply to any amendment thereto.

Article 38 (Disclosure of operations)
(1) The president shall prepare a business report and disclose the operational status of the cooperative as prescribed by the articles of incorporation. <Amended on December 27, 2022>
(2) The president shall keep the articles of incorporation, minutes of general meetings and meetings of the board of directors, and the membership register at the principal office. <Amended on December 27, 2022>
(3) Members and creditors of the cooperative may inspect the documents under paragraph (2) or request copies thereof. In such cases, they shall pay the costs prescribed by the cooperative.
(4) The president shall not refuse a request under paragraph (3) without good cause. <Amended on December 27, 2022>

Article 39 (Inspection of accounting books)
(1) A member may, at any time, request the president to permit inspection of accounting books and accounting-related documents or delivery of copies thereof, with the consent of at least one fifth of the members. <Amended on December 27, 2022>
(2) The president shall not refuse a request under paragraph (1) without good cause. <Amended on December 27, 2022>
(3) If there are grounds to suspect misconduct or a material violation of statutes or the articles of incorporation in the execution of the cooperative's affairs, members may, with the consent of at least 3/100 of the members, request a court to appoint an inspector to investigate the cooperative's affairs and financial condition. In such cases, Article 467 of the Commercial Act shall apply mutatis mutandis.

Article 40 (Statutory reserves, business reserves, and carryovers)
(1) A cooperative shall set aside at least one tenth of its surplus for each fiscal year (hereinafter referred to as "statutory reserves") until the amount prescribed by the articles of incorporation is reached.
(2) A cooperative may accumulate business reserves as prescribed by its articles of incorporation.
(3) Statutory reserves shall not be used for any purpose other than covering losses.
(4) To meet business expenses, a cooperative shall carry forward at least half of its surplus for each fiscal year to the following fiscal year.

Article 41 (Coverage of losses and distribution of surplus)
(1) When a cooperative incurs losses, it shall cover them in the order of undistributed carryovers, business reserves, and statutory reserves.
(2) A cooperative may not distribute the surplus for the relevant year unless it has first deducted amounts needed to cover losses from statutory reserves, business reserves, and undistributed carryovers.
(3) Surplus shall be distributed in proportion to members' capital contributions and their use of the cooperative's projects, as prescribed by the articles of incorporation.

Article 42 (Submission and keeping of settlement reports)
(1) The president shall submit a settlement report (meaning a business report, balance sheet, income statement, proposed disposition of surplus or proposed treatment of losses, etc.; hereinafter the same shall apply) to the auditor and keep it at the principal office at least one week before the regular general meeting. <Amended on December 27, 2022>
(2) Members and creditors of the cooperative may inspect the settlement report under paragraph (1) or request copies thereof. In such cases, they shall pay the costs prescribed by the cooperative.
(3) The president shall submit the settlement report under paragraph (1) and the auditor's opinion to the regular general meeting and obtain its approval. <Amended on December 27, 2022>
(4) Where approval is obtained under paragraph (3), Article 450 of the Commercial Act shall apply mutatis mutandis to the release of officers from liability.

Article 43 (Settlement of accounts)
A cooperative shall complete settlement of accounts for each fiscal year within two months after the end of the fiscal year; prepare a settlement report as prescribed by its articles of incorporation and obtain approval of the general meeting; publicly announce its balance sheet without delay; and submit its settlement report to the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries. <Amended on February 29, 2008; December 19, 2008; March 23, 2013>

Article 44 (Reduction of capital contributions and public notice)
(1) If a cooperative resolves to reduce the value of one capital contribution unit, it shall prepare an inventory of property and a balance sheet within two weeks.
(2) Within the period specified in paragraph (1), the cooperative shall publicly announce that creditors may file objections within a specified period and shall separately notify known creditors to that effect.
(3) The period for filing objections under paragraph (2) shall be at least 30 days.

Article 45 (Protection of creditors upon reduction of capital contributions)
(1) A creditor who does not file an objection within the period specified in Article 44 (3) shall be deemed to have approved the reduction in the value of one capital contribution unit.
(2) If a creditor files an objection, the cooperative shall discharge the obligation or furnish adequate security.

Article 46 (Prohibition of acquisition of equity interests)
A cooperative must not acquire a member's equity interest or accept such an interest as the subject of a pledge.

Article 47 (Application of surplus to capital contributions)
A member may apply surplus distributable to the member toward payment of the member's capital contribution, as prescribed by the articles of incorporation.

SECTION 6 DISSOLUTION

Article 48 (Dissolution)
(1) A cooperative shall be dissolved upon the occurrence of any of the following events: <Amended on February 29, 2008; December 19, 2008; March 23, 2013>
1. Resolution of the general meeting;
2. Bankruptcy of the cooperative;
3. Expiration of its period of existence or occurrence of a ground for dissolution prescribed by the articles of incorporation;
4. An order of dissolution issued by the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries under Article 52.
(2) When a cooperative is dissolved, it shall report the dissolution to the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries within two weeks from the date of dissolution. <Amended on February 29, 2008; December 19, 2008; March 23, 2013>

CHAPTER III SUPERVISION

Article 49 (Inspections)
(1) If the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries deems that the affairs or accounting of a cooperative violates a statute or its articles of incorporation or that its operation is improper, the Minister may require the cooperative to submit necessary reports on its affairs or accounting or direct public officials under the Minister's authority to inspect its affairs or accounting. <Amended on February 29, 2008; December 19, 2008; March 23, 2013>
(2) If members consider that the cooperative's affairs or accounting violates its articles of incorporation or regulations, they may request an inspection in writing from the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries with the consent of at least one third of all members. <Amended on February 29, 2008; December 19, 2008; March 23, 2013>
(3) Upon receipt of a request under paragraph (2), the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries shall inspect the affairs or accounting of the cooperative. <Amended on February 29, 2008; December 19, 2008; March 23, 2013>

Article 50 (Cancellation or suspension of execution of illegal or improper resolutions)
Where the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries considers that any matter resolved by the general meeting or board of directors of a cooperative concerning a project falling under any of the following subparagraphs (limited to where necessary to achieve a public purpose) is illegal or improper, the Minister may cancel all or part of the resolution or order suspension of its execution: <Amended on February 29, 2008; December 19, 2008; March 23, 2013>
1. Purchasing, storage, and sales projects for members;
2. Mutual aid projects for members;
3. Projects entrusted by the Government or public organizations.

Article 51 (Administrative dispositions against violations)
(1) If the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries deems that the cooperative's affairs or accounting specified in Article 50 violates any statute, an administrative disposition under a statute, or the articles of incorporation, the Minister may order the cooperative to rectify the violation within a specified period and require the following measures against the officers or employees concerned: <Amended on February 29, 2008; December 19, 2008; March 23, 2013>
1. Replacement of an officer or suspension of an officer's duties;
2. Dismissal, suspension from office, or reduction of salary for an employee.
(2) If a cooperative fails to comply with a corrective order or measures against officers or employees under paragraph (1), the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries may suspend all or some of its affairs for a specified period not exceeding six months. <Amended on February 29, 2008; December 19, 2008; March 23, 2013; May 21, 2014>
(3) Detailed standards for suspension of affairs under paragraph (2) and other necessary matters shall be prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Oceans and Fisheries. <Newly inserted on May 21, 2014>

Article 52 (Orders of dissolution)
If a cooperative fails to commence its affairs within one year from the date of authorization of establishment or continuously fails to carry on its affairs for at least one year without good cause, the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries may order the removal of its officers or the dissolution of the cooperative. <Amended on February 29, 2008; December 19, 2008; March 23, 2013>

Article 53 (Reports)
(1) The president shall report to the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries within two weeks after the occurrence of any of the following events, as prescribed by Presidential Decree: <Amended on February 29, 2008; December 19, 2008; March 23, 2013; December 27, 2022>
1. Holding of a general meeting;
2. Change of address;
3. Appointment of an officer;
4. Enactment, amendment, or repeal of regulations;
5. Change in membership.
(2) In addition to the reports under paragraph (1), the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries may order a cooperative to submit reports when necessary. <Amended on February 29, 2008; December 19, 2008; March 23, 2013>

Article 54 (Hearings)
The Minister of Oceans and Fisheries shall hold a hearing before ordering dissolution of a cooperative under Article 52. <Amended on February 29, 2008; December 19, 2008; March 23, 2013>

Article 55 (Subsidies)
To foster the salt industry, the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries may, within the budget for each fiscal year, subsidize all or part of the expenses necessary for establishing standards, inspections, or other State-subsidized projects of the cooperative. <Amended on February 29, 2008; December 19, 2008; March 23, 2013>

Article 56 (Invalidation of election due to election crimes by a successful candidate)
Where a person elected as president of a cooperative is sentenced to imprisonment or a fine of at least one million won for an offense under Article 59 committed in the relevant election, that person's election shall become invalid. <Amended on December 27, 2022>

CHAPTER V PENALTY PROVISIONS

Article 57 (Penalties)
(1) An officer who disposes of property of a cooperative beyond the scope of its projects shall be punished by imprisonment for not more than ten years or by a fine not exceeding 100 million won. <Amended on October 15, 2014>
(2) Both imprisonment and a fine under paragraph (1) may be imposed concurrently.

Article 58 (Penalties)
An officer, employee, or liquidator who commits any of the following acts shall be punished by imprisonment for not more than three years or by a fine not exceeding 30 million won: <Amended on February 29, 2008; December 19, 2008; March 23, 2013>
1. Improperly making a registration under Articles 49 through 52, 85, or 94 of the Civil Act as applied mutatis mutandis under Article 5;
2. Failing to obtain authorization from the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries in violation of Article 7 (3);
3. Executing a matter without a resolution of the general meeting (including the council of delegates where it resolves a matter in lieu of the general meeting under Article 16 (3)) or the board of directors as required by Article 12 or Article 19 (1);
4. Making a false report or concealing facts in violation of Article 413 of the Commercial Act as applied mutatis mutandis under Article 21 (5), (6), and (8);
5. Failing to obtain approval from the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries in violation of Article 33 (1);
6. Failing to keep the articles of incorporation, minutes of the general meeting and board of directors, and membership register in violation of Article 38 (2);
7. Refusing without good cause a request to inspect documents or obtain copies thereof in violation of Article 38 (4);
8. Refusing without good cause a request to inspect accounting books or accounting-related documents or obtain copies thereof in violation of Article 39 (2);
9. Failing to set aside at least one tenth of surplus in violation of Article 40 (1);
10. Using statutory reserves in violation of Article 40 (3);
11. Failing to carry forward at least one half of surplus for each fiscal year to the following fiscal year in violation of Article 40 (4);
12. Distributing surplus in violation of Article 41 (2);
13. Failing to submit or keep a settlement report in violation of Article 42 (1);
14. Failing to prepare an inventory of property and a balance sheet in violation of Article 44 (1);
15. Acquiring an equity interest or accepting it as the subject of a pledge in violation of Article 46;
16. Refusing, obstructing, or evading an inspection under Article 49 (1) or (3), or making a false report;
17. Violating a disposition or order of the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries under Articles 50 through 52;
18. Making a false report or concealing facts in violation of Article 53 (1);
19. Failing to submit a report or submitting a false report in violation of an order under Article 53 (2).

Article 59 (Penalties)
(1) A person who commits any act specified in Article 28 (1) shall be punished by imprisonment for not more than two years or by a fine not exceeding 20 million won.
(2) A person who falls under any of the following subparagraphs shall be punished by imprisonment for not more than one year or by a fine not exceeding ten million won:
1. A person who visits voters from house to house or assembles them at a specific place in violation of Article 28 (2);
2. A person who conducts election campaigning in violation of Article 28 (4);
3. A person who obstructs access, fails to comply with a demand for materials, or submits false materials in violation of Article 272-2 (3) of the Public Official Election Act as applied mutatis mutandis under Article 29 (2).
(3) A person who publishes false facts or publicly disparages a candidate by stating facts in violation of Article 28 (3) shall be punished by a fine of not less than five million won and not more than 30 million won.
(4) The statute of limitations for the prosecution of offenses under paragraphs (1) through (3) shall expire six months after the date of the relevant election (or, for an offense committed after the election date, six months after the date of the act): Provided, That the period shall be three years where an offender absconds, or causes an accomplice or a witness needed to prove the offense to abscond.

Article 60 (Administrative fines)
(1) A person who uses the name "salt industry cooperative" or any similar name in violation of Article 3 shall be subject to an administrative fine not exceeding two million won.
(2) An officer, employee, or liquidator of a cooperative who commits any of the following acts shall be subject to an administrative fine not exceeding two million won:
1. Neglecting registration under Articles 49 through 52, 85, or 94 of the Civil Act as applied mutatis mutandis under Article 5;
2. Neglecting to give public notice or notice to a creditor, or giving a false public notice or notice in violation of Article 44 (2).
(3) Administrative fines under paragraphs (1) and (2) shall be imposed and collected by the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries as prescribed by Presidential Decree. <Amended on February 29, 2008; December 19, 2008; March 23, 2013>
